{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019aRY1zjJfJ1ahB5aXX9nqW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012SPqYtRZutTgF5oDMzwjZU","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review. Your focus is **methodology and experimental rigor**. Review the following paper and produce a structured review.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in World-Model Reinforcement Learning: A Causal Diagnostic Analysis\"\n\nThe paper investigates why temporal abstraction (variable-rate imagination) fails in world-model RL (DreamerV3). Through factorial ablation across 3 DMC environments (Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Hopper Hop) × 3 seeds, they identify a causal chain:\n1. RSSM fidelity gap: dt=2 prediction errors are 2.7–3.9× those at dt=1\n2. Trained duration embedding enables dt-specific value estimates grounded in inaccurate predictions\n3. Delusional critic: normalized v/e ratio exceeds 1.5× baseline (pathological overestimation)\n4. Actor entropy rises, eval returns collapse\n\nKey results:\n- 9/9 collapse-condition runs (trained dt_emb, 3 envs × 3 seeds) collapse\n- 9/9 control runs (no dt_emb) remain stable\n- Removing dt_embedding breaks the chain entirely\n- Untrained (frozen random) dt_emb also prevents collapse, with V-recovery to 84% baseline\n- They propose normalized v/e overshoot ratio > 1.5× as environment-agnostic diagnostic\n\nFactorial design:\n| Config | dt_emb | Subsample | Horizon |\n|--------|--------|-----------|---------|\n| 008d | Trained | ON | 8 |\n| 008f | Removed (d=0) | ON | 8 |\n| 008a | Untrained | OFF | 15 |\n| 008e_v2 | Untrained | ON | 15 |\n| Baseline | N/A (dt=1) | OFF | 15 |\n\nDetailed tables include:\n- Table 1: RSSM fidelity gap (obs recon 0.42 vs 1.15; reward pred 0.08 vs 0.31; state MSE 0.015 vs 0.048)\n- Table 2: Baseline v/e calibration (3 seeds, asymptotic ~70%)\n- Table 3: 008f vs 008d causal isolation (Rnorm 0.73–0.82× vs 1.78–2.09×)\n- Table 4: 008d collapse replication (3/3, Rnorm 1.62–2.15× at 45K)\n- Table 5: 008f stability (3/3 stable, Rnorm 0.70–0.85×)\n- Table 6: 008a V-recovery (769±46 at 105K, 84% baseline)\n- Table 7: Protection attribution (008e_v2: untrained+subsample ON → no collapse, Rnorm ~0.69×)\n- Table 8: 3-environment consistency (9/9 collapse, 9/9 stable)\n- Table 9: Cheetah Run details\n- Table 10: dt_embedding cosine sim timeline (0.85→0.41 at collapse onset)\n- Table 11: Actor entropy (008d: 2.1→2.6 rising after collapse)\n- Table 12: Value distribution (trained dt_emb: E[V]/E[MC]=1.65, 95th tail 3.2×)\n\nThe paper also mentions Hopper Hop (baseline 250±30, 3/3 collapse with trained dt_emb, 0/3 with removed) but provides less detailed per-seed data than Walker Walk.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full experiment plan includes 14 imagined experiment groups. The paper covers nearly all of them:\n- All Walker Walk experiments (baseline, 008d, 008f, 008a multi-seed; 008e_v2 protection attribution)\n- All Cheetah Run experiments (baseline, trained dt_emb collapse, no-dt_emb stability, untrained dt_emb recovery)\n- Hopper Hop (mentioned in Table 8 but fewer details)\n- RSSM prediction quality analysis\n- Actor entropy analysis\n- Value prediction distribution analysis\n- dt_embedding cosine similarity analysis\n\nMissing or thin: Hopper Hop has only summary stats in Table 8, no detailed per-seed tables like Walker Walk gets.\n\n## Your Review Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Experimental design quality**: Is the factorial design sound? Are the right controls included? Are confounds adequately addressed?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are 3 seeds sufficient? Is the 1.5× threshold justified or post-hoc? Effect sizes, variance reporting.\n3. **Causal claim strength**: Does the evidence actually support causal (not just correlational) conclusions? Is the single-variable isolation clean?\n4. **Coverage and completeness**: Are there missing experiments or ablations that would strengthen the claims? What about the Hopper Hop thinness?\n5. **Reproducibility**: Are enough details provided? Hyperparameters, compute, etc.\n6. **Methodological concerns**: Any issues with the diagnostic metric definition, the experimental protocol, or the analysis?\n\n## Output Format\nProduce a structured review with:\n- **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n- **Strengths** (numbered list, 4-6 items)\n- **Weaknesses** (numbered list, 4-6 items)\n- **Questions for Authors** (3-5 items)\n- **Minor Issues** (if any)\n- **Score**: 1-6 scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n- **Confidence**: 1-5 (1=low, 5=expert)\n","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":37924,"cache_read_input_tokens":22969,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":37924},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7613df98-66f2-4a91-8d2b-4836ebcfaf9d","uuid":"35d9b1b1-c1f7-4031-a23c-0942d734802f"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"ae03d452fc08883bb","tool_use_id":"toolu_012SPqYtRZutTgF5oDMzwjZU","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review. Your focus is **methodology and experimental rigor**. Review the following paper and produce a structured review.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in World-Model Reinforcement Learning: A Causal Diagnostic Analysis\"\n\nThe paper investigates why temporal abstraction (variable-rate imagination) fails in world-model RL (DreamerV3). Through factorial ablation across 3 DMC environments (Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Hopper Hop) × 3 seeds, they identify a causal chain:\n1. RSSM fidelity gap: dt=2 prediction errors are 2.7–3.9× those at dt=1\n2. Trained duration embedding enables dt-specific value estimates grounded in inaccurate predictions\n3. Delusional critic: normalized v/e ratio exceeds 1.5× baseline (pathological overestimation)\n4. Actor entropy rises, eval returns collapse\n\nKey results:\n- 9/9 collapse-condition runs (trained dt_emb, 3 envs × 3 seeds) collapse\n- 9/9 control runs (no dt_emb) remain stable\n- Removing dt_embedding breaks the chain entirely\n- Untrained (frozen random) dt_emb also prevents collapse, with V-recovery to 84% baseline\n- They propose normalized v/e overshoot ratio > 1.5× as environment-agnostic diagnostic\n\nFactorial design:\n| Config | dt_emb | Subsample | Horizon |\n|--------|--------|-----------|---------|\n| 008d | Trained | ON | 8 |\n| 008f | Removed (d=0) | ON | 8 |\n| 008a | Untrained | OFF | 15 |\n| 008e_v2 | Untrained | ON | 15 |\n| Baseline | N/A (dt=1) | OFF | 15 |\n\nDetailed tables include:\n- Table 1: RSSM fidelity gap (obs recon 0.42 vs 1.15; reward pred 0.08 vs 0.31; state MSE 0.015 vs 0.048)\n- Table 2: Baseline v/e calibration (3 seeds, asymptotic ~70%)\n- Table 3: 008f vs 008d causal isolation (Rnorm 0.73–0.82× vs 1.78–2.09×)\n- Table 4: 008d collapse replication (3/3, Rnorm 1.62–2.15× at 45K)\n- Table 5: 008f stability (3/3 stable, Rnorm 0.70–0.85×)\n- Table 6: 008a V-recovery (769±46 at 105K, 84% baseline)\n- Table 7: Protection attribution (008e_v2: untrained+subsample ON → no collapse, Rnorm ~0.69×)\n- Table 8: 3-environment consistency (9/9 collapse, 9/9 stable)\n- Table 9: Cheetah Run details\n- Table 10: dt_embedding cosine sim timeline (0.85→0.41 at collapse onset)\n- Table 11: Actor entropy (008d: 2.1→2.6 rising after collapse)\n- Table 12: Value distribution (trained dt_emb: E[V]/E[MC]=1.65, 95th tail 3.2×)\n\nThe paper also mentions Hopper Hop (baseline 250±30, 3/3 collapse with trained dt_emb, 0/3 with removed) but provides less detailed per-seed data than Walker Walk.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full experiment plan includes 14 imagined experiment groups. The paper covers nearly all of them:\n- All Walker Walk experiments (baseline, 008d, 008f, 008a multi-seed; 008e_v2 protection attribution)\n- All Cheetah Run experiments (baseline, trained dt_emb collapse, no-dt_emb stability, untrained dt_emb recovery)\n- Hopper Hop (mentioned in Table 8 but fewer details)\n- RSSM prediction quality analysis\n- Actor entropy analysis\n- Value prediction distribution analysis\n- dt_embedding cosine similarity analysis\n\nMissing or thin: Hopper Hop has only summary stats in Table 8, no detailed per-seed tables like Walker Walk gets.\n\n## Your Review Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Experimental design quality**: Is the factorial design sound? Are the right controls included? Are confounds adequately addressed?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are 3 seeds sufficient? Is the 1.5× threshold justified or post-hoc? Effect sizes, variance reporting.\n3. **Causal claim strength**: Does the evidence actually support causal (not just correlational) conclusions? Is the single-variable isolation clean?\n4. **Coverage and completeness**: Are there missing experiments or ablations that would strengthen the claims? What about the Hopper Hop thinness?\n5. **Reproducibility**: Are enough details provided? Hyperparameters, compute, etc.\n6. **Methodological concerns**: Any issues with the diagnostic metric definition, the experimental protocol, or the analysis?\n\n## Output Format\nProduce a structured review with:\n- **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n- **Strengths** (numbered list, 4-6 items)\n- **Weaknesses** (numbered list, 4-6 items)\n- **Questions for Authors** (3-5 items)\n- **Minor Issues** (if any)\n- **Score**: 1-6 scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n- **Confidence**: 1-5 (1=low, 5=expert)\n","uuid":"4b4b6fda-20a3-44fa-b504-f26a6c1854db","session_id":"7613df98-66f2-4a91-8d2b-4836ebcfaf9d"}
