{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SmYC3ZJoVYcTDX6bAmx5ax","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my structured review as Reviewer R2 (Novelty, Significance, and Related Work).\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review: \"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in World-Model Reinforcement Learning: A Causal Diagnostic Analysis\"\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper presents a diagnostic investigation of why variable-rate imagination (temporal abstraction with dt>1) fails in DreamerV3. Through factorial ablation across 3 DMC environments and 3 seeds, the authors identify a causal chain: trained duration embeddings enable the critic to form dt-specific value estimates grounded in inaccurate RSSM predictions at dt>1, producing systematic value overestimation (termed \"delusional critic\") that drives policy collapse. The paper introduces a normalized value/eval overshoot ratio as a diagnostic metric and recommends against jointly training dt-embeddings with the value function under current RSSM architectures.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **The core finding is genuinely novel in its specificity.** While value overestimation in MBRL is well-known (Janner et al. 2019), the precise mechanism through which a *trained temporal conditioning variable* interacts with world-model fidelity gaps to produce delusional value estimates has not been previously identified. This is not merely \"model exploitation happens\"; it is a specific architectural diagnosis: the dt-embedding acts as a channel through which the critic can overfit to systematically biased multi-step predictions. This specificity is a real contribution.\n\n2. **The single-variable isolation (008d vs 008f) is methodologically clean.** The comparison where the only difference is dt_embed_dim=32 vs 0, with everything else held constant (subsample ON, H=8, same environment, same seeds), is the kind of controlled experiment that is rare in RL papers. The 9/9 vs 0/9 collapse split across three environments is compelling and the reported Cohen's d > 10 makes the effect unambiguous.\n\n3. **The normalized v/e overshoot ratio is a useful, if modest, contribution.** Normalizing by the baseline's own v/e trajectory at the same training step is a sensible way to remove environment-specific and training-phase-specific confounds. The 1.5x threshold achieving 100% separation across 18 runs is noteworthy. This metric could see adoption as a lightweight sanity check in future MBRL temporal abstraction work.\n\n4. **The paper is well-positioned as a diagnostic/negative-result paper.** The authors do not overclaim -- they explicitly state this is not a method paper. The framing as a \"closed causal chain\" with each link independently verified is appropriate and well-executed.\n\n5. **The mechanistic evidence (cosine similarity tracking, actor entropy, distributional analysis) adds depth.** The temporal coupling between embedding differentiation and collapse onset (Table 7) provides a satisfying mechanistic narrative beyond just correlating aggregate metrics.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **Related work coverage is significantly incomplete -- this is the paper's most serious deficit.** With only ~10 references, the paper misses several important lines of work:\n\n   - **Multi-step prediction and TD(k) literature**: The entire body of work on multi-step returns and their interaction with model error is absent. Buckman et al. (2018, \"Sample-Efficient Reinforcement Learning with Stochastic Ensemble Value Expansion\") and Feinberg et al. (2018, \"Model-Based Value Estimation for Efficient Model-Free RL\") directly address how model prediction horizon interacts with value estimation accuracy -- exactly the phenomenon studied here.\n   \n   - **Temporal difference learning at different timescales**: Romoff et al. (2019, \"Separating Value Functions Across Time-Scales\") study multi-timescale value learning and is directly relevant.\n   \n   - **Conservative value estimation in MBRL**: CQL (Kumar et al. 2020), COMBO (Yu et al. 2021), and MOPO (Yu et al. 2020) all address value overestimation in model-based or offline settings. The \"delusional critic\" phenomenon could potentially be mitigated by conservative estimation techniques -- this connection is unexplored.\n   \n   - **Model ensemble methods**: Kurutach et al. (2018, METRPO) and the ensemble approach in PETS (which the paper cites but does not discuss in this context) use ensembles specifically to combat model exploitation. The paper should discuss whether ensembling the RSSM at dt>1 could mitigate the fidelity gap.\n   \n   - **Hierarchical RL beyond options**: The paper cites only options, FiGAR, and option-critic. Missing are feudal networks (Vezhnevets et al. 2017), HAM (Parr & Russell 1997), MAXQ (Dietterich 2000), and goal-conditioned HRL (Nachum et al. 2018, HIRO; Levy et al. 2019). These provide alternative framings of temporal abstraction that do not require world-model imagination at coarse timescales.\n   \n   - **Recent temporal abstraction in MBRL (2023-2025)**: The paper does not cite any work on temporal abstraction in world models from the past 2-3 years. Director (Hafner et al. 2022) learns goal-conditioned hierarchical policies within DreamerV3 -- this is directly relevant as an alternative approach to temporal abstraction in the same architecture family. TWM (Robine et al. 2023) and IRIS (Micheli et al. 2023) are transformer-based world models where multi-step prediction may have different fidelity characteristics.\n   \n   - **The \"delusional policies\" citation appears incorrect.** Delalleau et al. (2019) is cited as the source for delusional policies, but the foundational work on delusional bias in RL is Lu et al. (2018, \"Non-delusional Q-learning and Value-iteration\") published at NeurIPS 2018, not the Delalleau workshop paper. This is a significant attribution error for a concept that is central to the paper's narrative.\n\n2. **The title and abstract overclaim relative to the evidence.** \"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in World-Model Reinforcement Learning\" implies generality across world-model architectures. The evidence is entirely from DreamerV3 with RSSM. Transformer-based world models (IRIS, TWM, TransDreamer), GNN-based models, or diffusion-based world models may have very different multi-step prediction characteristics. The causal chain is RSSM-specific (the fidelity gap at dt>1 is an RSSM property), and the title should reflect this. A more accurate title would be \"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in RSSM-Based World-Model RL\" or similar scoping.\n\n3. **The paper does not engage with the obvious next question: is this fixable?** The \"mitigation directions\" in Section 5.4 are hand-waved in a single paragraph listing four ideas with no analysis, no pilot experiments, and no theoretical grounding. For a diagnostic paper, the value proposition depends heavily on whether the diagnosis leads to actionable fixes. The recommendation \"don't train dt_embedding\" is actionable but trivially so -- it amounts to \"don't do temporal abstraction this way.\" A stronger paper would include at least one pilot mitigation (e.g., conservative value regularization, ensemble-based uncertainty-penalized values, or the decoupled training schedule mentioned) to demonstrate that the diagnosis enables progress.\n\n4. **Three DMC locomotion environments are not sufficient to claim \"environment-agnostic\" diagnostics.** Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, and Hopper Hop are all locomotion tasks with similar continuous control dynamics. The claim that R_norm > 1.5x is an \"environment-agnostic diagnostic\" is not supported without testing on structurally different tasks (manipulation, navigation, sparse-reward environments, or at minimum non-locomotion DMC tasks like Cartpole Swingup or Cup Catch). The 1.5x threshold may be specific to locomotion dynamics and reward structure.\n\n5. **Some reported numbers in the \"best\" paper version appear to be projections rather than completed experiments.** Cross-referencing the paper's claims with the project's memory.md and report.md reveals discrepancies. The paper reports 3-seed results for Cheetah Run and Hopper Hop (Tables 6-8), but the project overview shows only Walker Walk experiments were completed (exp_001 through exp_008f), with Cheetah Run listed as \"planned\" in the ROADMAP. The multi-seed tables for Cheetah Run and Hopper Hop may be projected/simulated rather than actually run. If so, this is a serious integrity concern. The paper should clearly distinguish completed from planned experiments.\n\n6. **The normalized v/e ratio, while useful, is not deeply analyzed for its limitations.** Under what conditions does it fail? If baseline performance is near-zero (early training, hard environments), the ratio becomes unstable. If the baseline itself has value overestimation issues (which some environments do), the normalization masks the problem. The paper presents the metric as if it were robust, but provides no failure mode analysis.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Can you confirm that the Cheetah Run and Hopper Hop multi-seed results (Tables 6-8) are from completed experiments, not projections?** The project roadmap lists these as \"planned\" with estimated GPU budgets. If these are simulated/projected, they must be clearly marked as such or removed.\n\n2. **Have you considered whether the delusional critic mechanism would manifest in transformer-based world models (IRIS, TWM) where multi-step prediction may degrade differently than RSSM?** If the fidelity gap is RSSM-specific, the paper's contribution is narrower than framed.\n\n3. **The \"delusional policies\" citation (Delalleau et al. 2019) does not appear to be the correct reference for this concept. Did you mean Lu et al. (2018, NeurIPS) \"Non-delusional Q-learning and Value-iteration\"?** This is important because the theoretical grounding of your \"delusional critic\" concept should connect to the correct prior work.\n\n4. **What happens if you apply standard MBRL overestimation mitigations (e.g., pessimistic value ensembles, truncated rollout horizons, or Dyna-style mixing of real and imagined data) to the dt>1 setting?** The paper diagnoses the problem but does not test whether known remedies work, which limits its practical impact.\n\n5. **The baseline v/e ratio converges to ~70%. This is substantial overestimation even at dt=1. How do you reconcile your \"delusional critic\" framing with the fact that the baseline critic also significantly overestimates?** Is the qualitative difference (collapse vs. no collapse) simply a matter of degree, or is there a phase transition?\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n- The bibliography formatting is inconsistent (some entries have arXiv identifiers, others do not).\n- Table 7 (cosine similarity) lacks error bars across seeds. Is the temporal coupling consistent across all 3 seeds?\n- The paper uses both \"dt\" and \"$\\Delta t$\" inconsistently between the text and equations.\n- Section 4.5 references \"008e_v2\" but the factorial grid table in Section 3.3 does not clearly explain the relationship between 008e_v2 and 008e_v3 mentioned in the project.\n- The claim \"Cohen's d exceeding 10\" should include the actual computed value, not just \"exceeding 10.\"\n\n### Score: 3 (Weak Reject)\n\nThe core diagnostic finding (trained dt-embedding as root cause of delusional critic in RSSM-based temporal abstraction) is novel and the single-variable isolation is methodologically strong. However, the paper has three issues that collectively push it below the acceptance threshold: (1) the related work coverage is inadequate for a paper whose primary contribution is positioning a phenomenon within the literature -- missing key references on multi-step value estimation, conservative MBRL, hierarchical RL, and potentially mis-attributing the delusional policies concept; (2) the generality claims in the title and abstract significantly exceed the evidence (3 locomotion tasks, 1 world-model architecture); and (3) the apparent discrepancy between claimed multi-environment multi-seed results and the project's actual experimental progress raises concerns about result integrity that must be resolved. A revision addressing these issues -- particularly completing the claimed experiments, expanding related work, and scoping claims to RSSM-based models -- could make this a solid contribution.\n\n### Confidence: 4 (High)\n\nI have substantial familiarity with the DreamerV3 architecture, MBRL value overestimation literature, temporal abstraction in RL, and the experimental methodology used here. I am less certain about the state of very recent (2025-2026) work on temporal abstraction in world models, which may contain directly relevant references I have not identified."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a6c0debdebea70408 (use SendMessage with to: 'a6c0debdebea70408' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 52211\ntool_uses: 17\nduration_ms: 136087</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7613df98-66f2-4a91-8d2b-4836ebcfaf9d","uuid":"b046e1e6-d01b-4630-9941-2b1b5c8138a4","timestamp":"2026-05-14T06:58:53.314Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review. Your focus is **novelty, significance, positioning, and related work**. Review the following paper and produce a structured review.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in World-Model Reinforcement Learning: A Causal Diagnostic Analysis\"\n\nThe paper investigates why temporal abstraction (variable-rate imagination) fails in world-model RL (DreamerV3). Through factorial ablation across 3 DMC environments (Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Hopper Hop) × 3 seeds, they identify a causal chain:\n1. RSSM fidelity gap: dt=2 prediction errors are 2.7–3.9× those at dt=1\n2. Trained duration embedding enables dt-specific value estimates grounded in inaccurate predictions\n3. Delusional critic: normalized v/e ratio exceeds 1.5× baseline (pathological overestimation)\n4. Actor entropy rises, eval returns collapse\n\nKey contributions claimed:\n1. Identification of the \"delusional critic\" mechanism specific to temporally-abstract world-model RL\n2. The normalized value/eval overshoot ratio as a reusable, environment-agnostic diagnostic tool\n3. A concrete architectural recommendation against joint dt-embedding training under current RSSM designs\n\nThe paper is positioned as a **diagnostic/negative-result** paper — it explains why something fails rather than proposing a new method that works.\n\n## Related Work Coverage in Paper\nThe paper cites:\n- Temporal abstraction: Sutton et al. 1999 (options), Sharma et al. 2017 (FiGAR), Bacon et al. 2017 (option-critic)\n- World-model RL: Hafner et al. 2020, 2021, 2023 (Dreamer series)\n- Value overestimation in MBRL: Janner et al. 2019 (MBPO)\n- Delusional policies: Delalleau et al. 2019\n- Other: Chua et al. 2018 (PETS), Lu et al. 2022 (offline MBRL design choices)\n\nTotal references: ~10\n\n## Key Technical Details\n- Built on DreamerV3 with RSSM world model\n- Duration embedding: learnable vector concatenated to RSSM state before critic input\n- Normalized v/e ratio: R_norm(t) = [V(t)/E(t)] / [V_base(t)/E_base(t)]\n- Pathological threshold: R_norm > 1.5×\n- 3 DMC environments, 3 seeds each, 5 configurations in factorial grid\n- 9/9 collapse with trained dt_emb, 9/9 stable without\n- Mechanistic evidence: cosine similarity of dt embeddings tracks collapse onset, actor entropy rises, value distribution right-tail inflated 3.2×\n\n## Your Review Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the \"delusional critic in temporally-abstract MBRL\" a genuinely new insight? How does it relate to known model exploitation phenomena (Janner et al. 2019)? Is the normalized v/e ratio truly novel as a diagnostic?\n2. **Significance**: How impactful is a negative-result/diagnostic paper? Who benefits from these findings? Is the recommendation (\"don't train dt_embedding\") actionable and useful?\n3. **Related work completeness**: Are important references missing? Consider:\n   - TD(k) and multi-step returns literature\n   - Temporal difference learning at different timescales (e.g., Romoff et al. 2019)\n   - Model predictive control with learned models\n   - Hierarchical RL beyond options (HAM, MAXQ, feudal networks, goal-conditioned HRL)\n   - Conservative value estimation (CQL, COMBO, etc.)\n   - Model ensemble methods for mitigating exploitation\n   - Recent temporal abstraction in MBRL (any 2023-2025 work?)\n4. **Positioning**: Is the paper well-positioned within the literature? Does it overclaim or underclaim?\n5. **Breadth of scope**: Is testing only on DreamerV3/RSSM + DMC sufficient to make general claims about \"temporal abstraction in world-model RL\"?\n6. **Writing quality and clarity**\n\n## Output Format\nProduce a structured review with:\n- **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n- **Strengths** (numbered list, 4-6 items)\n- **Weaknesses** (numbered list, 4-6 items)\n- **Questions for Authors** (3-5 items)\n- **Minor Issues** (if any)\n- **Score**: 1-6 scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n- **Confidence**: 1-5 (1=low, 5=expert)\n","agentId":"a6c0debdebea70408","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my structured review as Reviewer R2 (Novelty, Significance, and Related Work).\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review: \"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in World-Model Reinforcement Learning: A Causal Diagnostic Analysis\"\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper presents a diagnostic investigation of why variable-rate imagination (temporal abstraction with dt>1) fails in DreamerV3. Through factorial ablation across 3 DMC environments and 3 seeds, the authors identify a causal chain: trained duration embeddings enable the critic to form dt-specific value estimates grounded in inaccurate RSSM predictions at dt>1, producing systematic value overestimation (termed \"delusional critic\") that drives policy collapse. The paper introduces a normalized value/eval overshoot ratio as a diagnostic metric and recommends against jointly training dt-embeddings with the value function under current RSSM architectures.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **The core finding is genuinely novel in its specificity.** While value overestimation in MBRL is well-known (Janner et al. 2019), the precise mechanism through which a *trained temporal conditioning variable* interacts with world-model fidelity gaps to produce delusional value estimates has not been previously identified. This is not merely \"model exploitation happens\"; it is a specific architectural diagnosis: the dt-embedding acts as a channel through which the critic can overfit to systematically biased multi-step predictions. This specificity is a real contribution.\n\n2. **The single-variable isolation (008d vs 008f) is methodologically clean.** The comparison where the only difference is dt_embed_dim=32 vs 0, with everything else held constant (subsample ON, H=8, same environment, same seeds), is the kind of controlled experiment that is rare in RL papers. The 9/9 vs 0/9 collapse split across three environments is compelling and the reported Cohen's d > 10 makes the effect unambiguous.\n\n3. **The normalized v/e overshoot ratio is a useful, if modest, contribution.** Normalizing by the baseline's own v/e trajectory at the same training step is a sensible way to remove environment-specific and training-phase-specific confounds. The 1.5x threshold achieving 100% separation across 18 runs is noteworthy. This metric could see adoption as a lightweight sanity check in future MBRL temporal abstraction work.\n\n4. **The paper is well-positioned as a diagnostic/negative-result paper.** The authors do not overclaim -- they explicitly state this is not a method paper. The framing as a \"closed causal chain\" with each link independently