{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"ae03d452fc08883bb","tool_use_id":"toolu_012SPqYtRZutTgF5oDMzwjZU","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":109079,"tool_uses":18,"duration_ms":159579},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"4f6e5792-d7e7-47ad-839d-cf12703710aa","session_id":"7613df98-66f2-4a91-8d2b-4836ebcfaf9d"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01UkMax9DUnJ7WEP3k3KkSdV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01D3KYRiZMhzVjJpNYpQ1rvN","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"\n# R1 Review: Methodology and Experimental Rigor\n\n## Paper\n\"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in World-Model Reinforcement Learning: A Causal Diagnostic Analysis\"\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nThis paper presents a factorial ablation study diagnosing why variable-rate imagination fails in DreamerV3. The authors identify a four-link causal chain: RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 --> trained duration embedding enables dt-specific value estimates --> delusional critic (systematic overestimation) --> actor entropy rise and performance collapse. The core experimental contribution is the 008d vs. 008f single-variable isolation (trained vs. removed dt_embedding), replicated across 3 DMC environments x 3 seeds with 9/9 collapse and 9/9 stability consistency. A normalized v/e overshoot ratio >1.5x is proposed as an environment-agnostic diagnostic.\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n1. **Clean single-variable isolation (008d vs. 008f).** The smoking-gun comparison holds subsample ON and H=8 constant, varying only dt_embed_dim (32 vs. 0). This is the strongest form of causal evidence available without formal interventional calculus. The effect size is enormous (Rnorm 1.71x vs. 0.73x at 35K; Cohen's d reportedly >10), making the conclusion robust even at modest sample sizes.\n\n2. **Multi-link causal chain with independent verification at each step.** The paper does not merely show correlation between dt_embedding and collapse. It verifies each link separately: RSSM fidelity gap (Table 1), embedding differentiation timeline via cosine similarity (Table 10), value distribution right-tail inflation (Table 12), and actor entropy rise (Table 11). This is substantially more rigorous than most diagnostic analyses in MBRL.\n\n3. **Thoughtful confound resolution.** The 008c/008d comparison initially confounded dt_embedding training with RSSM training data composition (subsample ON/OFF). The authors recognized this and designed 008f specifically to resolve it. The protection attribution experiment (008e_v2) further disentangles untrained dt_emb from subsample setting. This iterative confound-hunting is methodologically exemplary.\n\n4. **The normalized v/e overshoot ratio is well-defined and principled.** Normalizing by baseline v/e at the same training step controls for the natural increase in v/e during training (baseline asymptotes at ~70%). Without this normalization, raw v/e >50% would be flagged as pathological when it is actually healthy baseline behavior. The metric design shows careful thinking.\n\n5. **Binary consistency of the core result.** 9/9 collapse-condition runs collapse; 9/9 control runs are stable. Zero misclassification by the 1.5x threshold across three environments. Even accounting for the small sample size, this level of consistency is compelling for a mechanistic claim.\n\n6. **Honest negative-result framing.** The paper started as an attempt to build temporal abstraction and pivoted to diagnosing why it fails. The transparency about this trajectory (visible in the experiment numbering 001-008f) adds credibility.\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n1. **Critical: A large fraction of the reported results appear to be predictions rather than actual experimental data.** Cross-referencing the paper's tables against the experiment registry and dream simulation files reveals a serious concern. The registry shows completed single-seed runs only for Walker Walk (exp_001 baseline, exp_006, exp_007, exp_008a, exp_008c, exp_008d). Experiments 008f and 008e_v3 are listed as RUNNING with data only up to 35K and 45K respectively. The multi-seed Walker Walk data (seeds 1-2 for baseline, 008d, 008f, 008a) all appear in the dream simulation's \"imagined_experiments.yaml\" with entries explicitly labeled \"(pred)\". All Cheetah Run data and all Hopper Hop data originate from the same dream simulation. The paper presents these predicted values in tables without any distinction from real data. If this paper were submitted with simulated/predicted data presented as empirical results, it would be grounds for desk rejection. **The authors must clearly indicate which results are from completed experiments and which are projections, or -- far preferably -- must actually run all claimed experiments before submission.**\n\n2. **Three seeds is the bare minimum, and the 1.5x threshold is post-hoc.** The threshold of Rnorm > 1.5x was derived from observing the separation between collapsed and stable runs, then retroactively declared as a diagnostic criterion. No independent validation set or held-out environment was used to validate it. With only 3 seeds per condition, the precise threshold value is not well-determined -- a bootstrap or leave-one-out analysis would help assess sensitivity. The paper should acknowledge this threshold as empirically observed rather than principled, and discuss what range of thresholds (e.g., 1.3x to 1.8x) would yield the same binary classification.\n\n3. **The factorial design has missing cells and unbalanced factors.** The five configurations in the factorial table (008d, 008f, 008a, 008e_v2, baseline) do not form a complete 2x2x2 grid. Notable missing configurations: (a) Trained dt_emb + subsample OFF + H=15 -- this would test whether trained dt_emb causes collapse even without subsample-induced training data changes; (b) Removed dt_emb + subsample OFF + H=15 -- the \"pure removal\" control at full horizon. Additionally, 008a and 008e_v2 use H=15 while 008d and 008f use H=8, confounding horizon length with the dt_emb comparison across these pairs. The paper acknowledges this implicitly but does not discuss the implications.\n\n4. **RSSM fidelity gap analysis (Table 1) lacks methodological detail.** How were the dt=1 vs. dt=2 losses measured? From the same trained model evaluated on held-out transitions? Which model checkpoint (what training step)? Is the fidelity gap measured on the 008d model (subsample ON, which trains on dt=2 transitions) or a different model? The fidelity gap is the foundational claim of the causal chain, yet its measurement methodology is insufficiently described. Furthermore, only Walker Walk numbers are reported -- are the ratios similar for Cheetah Run and Hopper Hop?\n\n5. **Value/eval ratio as an overestimation proxy has known limitations.** V(t)/E(t) conflates two quantities: the critic's accuracy and the agent's policy quality. A high V/E could arise from (a) the critic overestimating, or (b) the policy degrading while the critic has not yet adjusted, or (c) eval return variance. The distributional analysis (Table 12) partially addresses this by comparing V(s) to MC returns directly, but that analysis has no reported methodology: how were MC returns computed? Over what episodes? The E[V]/E[MC] = 1.65 for trained dt_emb is more informative than the v/e ratio but is presented as a secondary analysis with minimal detail.\n\n6. **Hopper Hop data is thin.** Table 8 claims 3/3 collapse and 3/3 stable for Hopper Hop, but no per-seed tables, no Rnorm trajectories, and no detailed metrics are provided anywhere in the paper for this environment. Given that the Hopper Hop data appears to originate entirely from dream simulation predictions (see Weakness 1), this environment contributes to the \"9/9\" headline number without contributing real evidence. If the Hopper Hop results are genuine but simply not shown in detail, per-seed data should be included in an appendix. If they are projections, they should be removed from the main claims entirely.\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Which results in the paper are from completed experiments vs. predictions/projections?** The multi-seed data for Walker Walk (seeds 1-2), all Cheetah Run data, and all Hopper Hop data appear to originate from a \"dream simulation\" rather than actual training runs. Can the authors confirm which tables contain real experimental data and which contain projected values? If projections are included, was any validation performed comparing dream predictions to actual outcomes for the experiments that were subsequently run?\n\n2. **How sensitive is the 1.5x threshold to the choice of baseline?** The normalized v/e ratio depends critically on the baseline v/e trajectory. If the baseline had a different v/e curve (e.g., in a harder environment where baseline v/e asymptotes lower), would 1.5x still be the right threshold? Have you tested threshold sensitivity -- e.g., what is the minimum threshold that still achieves 100% separation?\n\n3. **Can you provide the RSSM fidelity gap measurement methodology?** Specifically: which model's weights were used, at what training step, on what evaluation data (held-out episodes?), and how were dt=2 transitions constructed for a model trained with subsample OFF vs. ON? This is important because the fidelity gap magnitude depends on whether the RSSM has been trained on dt=2 transitions.\n\n4. **What happens with trained dt_embedding but explicit value regularization?** The paper recommends against joint training of dt_embedding, but this is a strong negative conclusion. Have the authors considered whether simple interventions (e.g., value function clipping, pessimistic value estimation, or target network momentum adjustments) could allow trained dt_embedding to work? Even a negative result from one such intervention would strengthen the paper's claim that the problem is fundamental rather than easily fixable.\n\n5. **The cosine similarity analysis (Table 10) shows Rnorm at 25K = 1.78x but at 35K = 1.71x -- a decrease. Can you explain this non-monotonicity?** If the delusional critic is accelerating, the normalized ratio should be monotonically increasing. The decrease from 1.78x to 1.71x before jumping to 2.09x at 45K suggests possible measurement noise or a more complex dynamic than the simple \"accelerating divergence\" narrative suggests.\n\n---\n\n## Minor Issues\n\n- The citation for \"delusional policies\" (Delalleau et al., 2019) appears to be a NeurIPS Workshop paper about discrete-continuous mixtures in MARL, not a paper about delusional policies. The correct reference for delusional policies is likely Lu et al. (2018, ICML) \"Non-delusional Q-learning and Value-Iteration.\" This should be verified and corrected.\n\n- Table 3 (causal isolation) reports 008f Rnorm@45K = 0.77x and @55K = 0.82x, but these are labeled \"(pred)\" in the source data. The paper presents them without qualification.\n\n- The paper uses \"collapse\" without a formal definition. Is collapse defined as Rnorm > 1.5x, or as eval return declining for N consecutive checkpoints, or something else? A precise operational definition would improve reproducibility.\n\n- Section 3.2 describes the duration embedding as \"concatenated to the RSSM state before input to the critic.\" It would help to clarify whether this also affects the actor network, and whether the embedding is also used during RSSM rollout (world model predictions) or only for value estimation.\n\n- Hyperparameter details are sparse. Learning rates, optimizer settings, network architectures, batch sizes, replay buffer details, and any DreamerV3 modifications beyond the dt-embedding/subsampling machinery are not reported. A hyperparameter table or appendix is needed for reproducibility.\n\n- The paper mentions 105K-112K training steps as the longest runs. This is quite short for DreamerV3 (original paper uses 500K-1M steps). The baseline reaches asymptotic performance, but it is unclear whether the collapse/stability conclusions would hold if training continued much longer -- e.g., does the untrained dt_emb configuration eventually also collapse at 500K steps?\n\n---\n\n## Score: 3 (Weak Reject)\n\nThe core experimental idea -- single-variable isolation of dt_embedding as the cause of delusional critic -- is sound and the effect is large. However, the paper has a critical credibility issue: a substantial portion of the reported results (multi-seed data, Cheetah Run, Hopper Hop) appear to be predictions from a simulation framework rather than actual experimental outcomes, yet are presented as empirical findings. The \"9/9 collapse, 9/9 stable\" headline is built on a foundation that includes predicted data. If the authors can run all claimed experiments and confirm the predictions, this paper would likely merit acceptance (score 4-5). Additionally, the post-hoc threshold, incomplete factorial grid, and thin methodological descriptions for key analyses (RSSM fidelity gap, value distribution, MC returns) need attention. The underlying insight is valuable and the causal reasoning is strong, but the paper as submitted does not meet the evidentiary standard its claims require.\n\n## Confidence: 4 (High)\n\nI have carefully cross-referenced the paper's claimed results against the project's experiment registry and dream simulation artifacts. I am confident in the assessment that a significant portion of the reported data is projected rather than empirical. My domain knowledge in MBRL, DreamerV3 architectures, and causal experimental design is strong. The one point of uncertainty is whether additional experiments were run after the registry snapshot I examined, which could change the assessment of Weakness 1 significantly.\n","project":"event-gated-adaptive-horizon","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260514T062606","overall_score":"Weak Reject (3/6)"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":854,"cache_read_input_tokens":107762,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":854,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012SPqYtRZutTgF5oDMzwjZU","session_id":"7613df98-66f2-4a91-8d2b-4836ebcfaf9d","uuid":"5d99df15-d0f6-49f1-8b86-5f032c98d3b5"}
